// BorrowCheck — **E3070: A COLLECTION MAY NOT BE MUTATED WHILE AN ELEMENT BORROWED OUT OF IT IS
// STILL LIVE.**
//
// `arr.get(i)` hands the caller the element POINTER and takes no reference for it
// (`Parser.emitContainerElementAccessor`, `ContainerElementReference.borrowed`) — the array stays the sole owner and its
// `__managed_decref` walk destroys the element. So a write that shortens or replaces the array
// (`clear`, `push` past capacity, a rebind of the binding itself) frees a record the caller is
// still holding: `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise ""` + `arr.clear()` + `print("{s}")`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS THE SAME WRITE DOORS THE ITERATION LOCK ALREADY HARDENED, ASKING A SECOND QUESTION
// OF THE SAME BINDING.** The lock asks *"is this binding being iterated?"*; the borrow set asks
// *"is an element of it still referenced?"*. Both are answered from ONE derivation of **which
// storage a write goes through** — `Parser.iterationSubjectNameAt` — so a chain shape taught to one
// is taught to the other, and a write cannot be locked under one key and checked under another; see
// that function's ⚠ block.
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE FACTS ARE RECORDED BY THE PARSER.** A borrow cannot be keyed on a var SLOT, because
// there are no `varDecl`/`varLoad`/`varStore` ops: `let`/`var` bind a name to the initializer's SSA
// `ValueId` in `Scope` at PARSE TIME (docs/internals/names-and-values.md, Names, SSA and phis), so `let x = 42` mints
// no op at all and an IR pass cannot see the bindings. The facts are therefore RECORDED BY THE
// PARSER, which is the only place they exist, and DECIDED here, which is the only place the
// whole-program parameter-mutation summary exists.
//
// Three consequences:
//
//   • **Liveness is EXACT, not approximated.** A use of a binding IS a `Scope` resolution
//     (`Parser.requireBindingLive`, the one read chokepoint), and the token cursor is monotone in
//     source order through a function body, so the last use needs no stand-in such as "the highest
//     physical op index this slot appeared at".
//   • **`pop`/`remove` are excluded STRUCTURALLY, not by name.** The mint sits at the one door that
//     already decides borrow-vs-move-out (`emitContainerElementAccessor`'s `owned` flag), so no
//     hand-maintained list of mutating method names is needed here —
//     `Parser.arrayMethodMutatesReceiver` is the ONE such list and it already exists.
//   • **NOTHING IS RECORDED UNTIL A FUNCTION ACTUALLY BORROWS.** Every mutation door reads
//     `borrows.count()` first, and a function with no live borrow answers 0 after one read and
//     allocates nothing — its `BorrowFacts` is still the shared empty. Rebuilding an active set at
//     every op or every call site would allocate on programs that borrow nothing.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A BORROW COMPOSES THROUGH A BORROW, AND NOT THROUGH A CALL — the two halves of "the borrow set
// does not compose".**
//
//   • **THROUGH A BORROW: closed here**, at `composePendingBorrowOntoBases`. An element read out of storage
//     that is itself borrowed is a borrow of that storage's base too, to the root. It is a real
//     use-after-free, and the mint is the only place with the binding in hand.
//   • **THROUGH A CALL: not modelled HERE — and it IS a hazard, closed at the argument door instead
//     (`Parser.anchorBorrowedArguments`).**
//
//     The argument below covers exactly `arr.last()` — an OWNED opaque return. It says nothing
//     about `arr.get(0)`, which is a BORROW (`ContainerElementReference.borrowed`), and nothing at
//     all about a managed FIELD read, which this file does not model. Handed to a call that can
//     also reach the storage they came out of — `a.replace(a.s)`, `clobber(b, borrowed: b.s)`,
//     `clobber(a, borrowed: try a.get(0) otherwise "")` — each is a use-after-free.
//
//     ⭐ **AND IT IS A RETAIN RATHER THAN AN E3070.** The conflict this file reports is one the
//     parser can PROVE — a NAMED borrow live across a write whose callee the whole-program summary
//     says writes that parameter. The argument shape is not provable at the same width: the callee
//     may reach the storage through a module-level `var`, where no mask carries the fact, so a rule
//     that refused only what it can prove would leave the rest as a use-after-free. It also has no
//     borrower NAME to blame, and `borrowConflictMessage` names one. So the caller HOLDS A
//     REFERENCE for the length of the statement and the program runs. Where it IS provable it is
//     refused: `specs/borrow-liveness.md`'s `mutating-callee-argument` gets E3070, because there
//     the borrow outlives the statement under a name.
//
//     Of its own subject: `arr.last()` is served from `stdlib/Array.maxon`, whose
//     `returns Element` is a borrowed opaque return, so the CALLEE discharges a real `+1`
//     (`Parser.emitOwnedValueReturn` → `coOwnBorrowedOpaque`) and the caller holds an owned value
//     that cannot dangle. What is missing THERE is a LANGUAGE RULE: `/specs/borrow-checker.md` pins
//     E3070 on that program, and `arr.last()` under a live write is ACCEPTED here.
//     `Parser.arraySurfaceMemberNames`' first bullet carries the design of the column that closes
//     it; it is the half `get`/`first` retire behind.
//
//     ⚖ **THE SCOPE IS THE WHOLE CORPUS-SERVED FAMILY, NOT `arr.last()` (user ruling).**
//     `List.first`/`get`/`clear` are corpus-served, so a write under their borrows is not refused
//     here; the cases `list/memory.value-survives-clear{,-error,-and-return}`,
//     `borrow-liveness/a-method-rebinding-a-list-field-frees-the-borrowed-element` and
//     `borrow-liveness/a-field-store-in-a-callee-freeing-its-parameters-list` pin that those
//     programs compile, run correctly and leak nothing.
//
//     ⇒ **The line is WHO MINTS THE BORROW, and it is not about the container.** `arr.get(0)` +
//     `arr.clear()` is refused, because `Parser.dispatchArrayMethod` mints that borrow in the
//     compiler; the same two operations on a `List` are not, because a call is what serves them.
//     **So a container joins this divergence exactly when it is retired into the corpus**, and the
//     next retirement moves its accessors the same way with no edit here — which is the reason this
//     bullet states the family and not a method.
//
// ⚠ **A LOOP MAKES SOURCE ORDER LIE.** A use textually BEFORE a mutation but inside a loop the borrow
// was bound before happens after it on the next trip, so a borrow is live to the end of every such loop
// (`StorageProvenance.liveUntilAcrossLoops`).

// The per-function id of a STORAGE a borrow reads an element of and a write goes through. Dense, and
// in practice tiny — it counts the DISTINCT such storages of one function body — but the range is WIDE
// because nothing CAPS it: a narrow bound here would be a runtime panic on a large machine-generated
// body rather than a diagnostic, and a bound is narrowed only when it is provable.
//
// ⭐ It is minted by resolving a NAME to its BINDING at the instant of the record
// (`Parser.borrowSubjectBindingOf`), so it carries the two properties a name string cannot: a body
// that SHADOWS the source's name declares a different `VarInfo` and gets a different subject
// (correctly writable), and a self-field's two spellings (`items`, `self.items`) reach the ONE alias
// `VarInfo` and so share one subject.
//
// ⚠ **THE SUBJECT SPACE IS WIDER THAN `Scope`.** A top-level `var` is storage a body borrows out of
// and writes exactly as it does a local, and it has no `VarInfo` anywhere — so one is INTERNED per
// name for the file (`VarInfo.createModuleStorageSubject`). Leaving module storage out was a
// reachable use-after-free on four spellings; `specs/borrow-liveness.md`'s `module-storage-*`
// cases pin each of them.
export typealias BorrowSubjectId = int(0 to u64.max) implements ElementIndex

export enum BorrowSubjectError implements Error
	namesNoStorage
end 'BorrowSubjectError'

export union BorrowSubject
	none
	storage(name ByteArray)

	export function isStorage() returns bool
		return match self 'subject'
			none gives false
			storage gives true
		end 'subject'
	end 'isStorage'

	export function storageName() returns ByteArray throws BorrowSubjectError
		match self 'subject'
			none then throw BorrowSubjectError.namesNoStorage
			storage(name) then return name
		end 'subject'
	end 'storageName'

	export function names(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return match self 'subject'
			none gives false
			storage(held) gives held.equals(name)
		end 'subject'
	end 'names'
end 'BorrowSubject'

export union MutationSiteWrite
	settledAtTheParse
	movedOut
	droppedAtBlockEnd

	// `argColumn` is the argument's index in the call op's OWN columns (a prepended method receiver
	// included), which is the index `argSlotPosition` maps to a parameter position.
	throughCall(callee ByteArray, argColumn ParamCount, argLabel ByteArray)
end 'MutationSiteWrite'

// @generated library-cache begin
function cacheWriteMutationSiteWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value MutationSiteWrite)
	writer.unsigned(value.ordinal as CacheUnsigned)

	match value 'payload'
		settledAtTheParse then break 'payload'
		movedOut then break 'payload'
		droppedAtBlockEnd then break 'payload'
		throughCall(a0, a1, a2) then cacheWriteMutationSiteWriteThroughCall(writer, a0: a0, a1: a1, a2: a2)
	end 'payload'
end 'cacheWriteMutationSiteWrite'

function cacheWriteMutationSiteWriteThroughCall(writer LibraryCacheWriter, a0 ByteArray, a1 ParamCount, a2 ByteArray)
	writer.bytes(a0)
	writer.unsigned(a1 as CacheUnsigned)
	writer.bytes(a2)
end 'cacheWriteMutationSiteWriteThroughCall'

function cacheReadMutationSiteWrite(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns MutationSiteWrite throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let tag = try reader.unsigned()

	return match tag 'case'
		0 gives MutationSiteWrite.settledAtTheParse
		1 gives MutationSiteWrite.movedOut
		2 gives MutationSiteWrite.droppedAtBlockEnd
		3 gives try cacheReadMutationSiteWriteThroughCall(reader)
		default throws LibraryCacheDamage.unknownTag(tag)
	end 'case'
end 'cacheReadMutationSiteWrite'

function cacheReadMutationSiteWriteThroughCall(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns MutationSiteWrite throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let a0 = try reader.bytes()
	let a1 = (try reader.unsigned()) as ParamCount
	let a2 = try reader.bytes()

	return MutationSiteWrite.throughCall(a0, argColumn: a1, argLabel: a2)
end 'cacheReadMutationSiteWriteThroughCall'

// @generated library-cache end

// The `via '<x>'` text for a mutation that is a REBIND of the source binding rather than a method
// call on it (`arr = other`, `items = other`, `self.items = other`): the write is named with the
// operator the user wrote.
export let ReassignMutationDisplay = b"="

export let BranchGiveMoveDisplay = b"the value its branch gives"
export let ClosureCaptureMoveDisplay = b"the closure that captures it"
export let ConsumingArgumentMoveDisplay = b"a consuming argument"
export let ContainerFeedMoveDisplay = b"the container or call it is fed to"
export let DurableSlotMoveDisplay = b"the storage it is stored into"

// ⭐ **AN ELEMENT BORROW THE PARSER HAS MINTED BUT NOT YET BOUND TO A NAME.** A borrow is minted at
// the ACCESSOR (`arr.get(0)`) and completed at the BINDING (`let s = …`), which is where the
// borrowing name and the activation point exist; an accessor whose result no binding takes is never
// completed and is dropped.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS KEYED ON THE VALUE THE ACCESSOR PRODUCED, and that is not a detail — it is the only
// key that answers "did the element actually reach this name?".** Two measured defects come from
// keying on POSITION instead:
//
//   • `let n = sizeOf(try arr.get(0) otherwise "")` binds an INTEGER computed from the element. The
//     element reaches no name and nothing can dangle, but a position key hands the borrow to `n` and
//     refuses the program — as it does to a counted range's loop variable, which is the header phi.
//   • `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise return 1` parses its handler by RE-ENTERING the statement
//     parser mid-initializer, so a position key that resets per statement is wiped there: NO borrow
//     is recorded at all and the following `arr.clear()` is waved through. Measured **0xC0000005**.
//
// A value id is unique within a function, so a stale pending borrow can never be claimed by a later
// binding and no per-statement reset is needed for correctness at all.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPLETION IS WHAT MAKES A MUTATION INSIDE A `try … otherwise` HANDLER LEGAL.** On the
// handler's path the get FAILED and the borrowing variable was
// never bound, so there is no borrow to conflict with — and since the handler is parsed BEFORE the
// binding completes, its tokens sit below the ACTIVATION and the ordering says so by construction.
// Activating at the CALL instead would refuse `specs/borrow-checker.md:borrow-not-live-in-otherwise`.
export type PendingBorrow
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId

	// The SSA value the accessor defined. A binding claims this borrow iff the value it binds IS this
	// one — after `Parser.retargetPendingBorrow` has moved it to the merge phi, which is what a
	// `try … otherwise <value>` actually binds.
	export let borrowValue as ValueId

	// The accessor's own line — what the diagnostic reports as `borrowed at line N`.
	export let borrowLine as LineNumber

	export static function create(subject BorrowSubjectId, borrowValue ValueId, borrowLine LineNumber) returns PendingBorrow
		return Self{subject: subject, borrowValue: borrowValue, borrowLine: borrowLine}
	end 'create'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		writer.unsigned(self.subject as CacheUnsigned)
		writer.unsigned(self.borrowValue as CacheUnsigned)
		writer.unsigned(self.borrowLine as CacheUnsigned)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns PendingBorrow throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_subject = (try reader.unsigned()) as BorrowSubjectId
		let f_borrowValue = (try reader.unsigned()) as ValueId
		let f_borrowLine = (try reader.unsigned()) as LineNumber

		return Self{subject: f_subject, borrowValue: f_borrowValue, borrowLine: f_borrowLine}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize24
		return sizeof(PendingBorrow) as CacheSize24
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'PendingBorrow'

export typealias PendingBorrowArray = Array with PendingBorrow

// @generated library-cache begin
function cacheWritePendingBorrowArray(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value PendingBorrowArray)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		item.cacheWrite(writer)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWritePendingBorrowArray'

function cacheReadPendingBorrowArray(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns PendingBorrowArray throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = PendingBorrowArray.create()
	result.reserve(count)

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachItem'
		let item = try PendingBorrow.cacheRead(reader)
		result.push(item)
	end 'eachItem'

	return result
end 'cacheReadPendingBorrowArray'

// @generated library-cache end

// A length of, or an index into, the pending-claim list. It is one STATEMENT long
// (`Parser.dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows`) and in practice 0 or 1, but nothing CAPS it — a statement may
// spell as many element accessors as it likes — so the range stays wide until one is provable.
export typealias PendingBorrowCount = int(0 to u64.max) implements ElementIndex

// A completed borrow: binding `borrower` holds an element of subject `subject` from `activationToken`
// until its own last use.
export type BorrowRecord
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId

	// ⭐ **THE BORROWING BINDING, HELD BY REFERENCE** — the same discipline
	// `Parser.iterationLockedBindings` states, and here for a second reason: the binding's
	// `lastUseToken` is written by `Parser.requireBindingLive` long AFTER this record is made, so a
	// name copied out at record time would be a second fact free to disagree with the binding it
	// describes. `VarInfo` is a heap `type`, so this record observes every later use.
	export let borrower as VarInfo

	// The token at which the borrowing name came into scope. A write BELOW it — a `try` handler's,
	// or anything earlier in the body — cannot conflict, because the borrow does not exist there yet.
	export let activationToken as ElementIndex
	export let borrowLine as LineNumber

	export static function create(subject BorrowSubjectId, borrower VarInfo, activationToken ElementIndex, borrowLine LineNumber) returns BorrowRecord
		return Self{subject: subject, borrower: borrower, activationToken: activationToken, borrowLine: borrowLine}
	end 'create'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		writer.unsigned(self.subject as CacheUnsigned)
		self.borrower.cacheWrite(writer)
		writer.unsigned(self.activationToken as CacheUnsigned)
		writer.unsigned(self.borrowLine as CacheUnsigned)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns BorrowRecord throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_subject = (try reader.unsigned()) as BorrowSubjectId
		let f_borrower = try VarInfo.cacheRead(reader)
		let f_activationToken = (try reader.unsigned()) as ElementIndex
		let f_borrowLine = (try reader.unsigned()) as LineNumber

		return Self{subject: f_subject, borrower: f_borrower, activationToken: f_activationToken, borrowLine: f_borrowLine}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize32
		return sizeof(BorrowRecord) as CacheSize32
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'BorrowRecord'

export typealias BorrowRecordArray = Array with BorrowRecord

// @generated library-cache begin
function cacheWriteBorrowRecordArray(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value BorrowRecordArray)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		item.cacheWrite(writer)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWriteBorrowRecordArray'

function cacheReadBorrowRecordArray(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns BorrowRecordArray throws LibraryCacheDamage
